{
 "id": 8,
 "number": 271,
 "title": "Encode and Decode Strings",
 "slug": "encode-and-decode-strings",
 "category": "arrays-hashing",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/encode-and-decode-strings/",
 "pattern": "Length prefix + delimiter",
 "statement": "Design `encode`, which packs a list of strings into **one** string, and `decode`, which turns that string back into the original list. The strings may contain **any** characters — including whatever delimiter you choose.",
 "example": {
  "input": "Codec()\nencode(['lee', 'code', '#hi'])  →  \"3#lee4#code3##hi\"\ndecode(\"3#lee4#code3##hi\")  →  [\"lee\",\"code\",\"#hi\"]",
  "output": "",
  "why": "Each word is written as its length, a #, then the word — so even a word containing # is read back correctly."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "Codec()\nencode([''])  →  \"0#\"\ndecode(\"0#\")  →  [\"\"]",
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "input": "Codec()\nencode(['a#b', '12#', ''])  →  \"3#a#b3#12#0#\"\ndecode(\"3#a#b3#12#0#\")  →  [\"a#b\",\"12#\",\"\"]",
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Simply joining with a separator such as `,` breaks as soon as a string contains that separator.",
  "Instead write each string as **its length, then `#`, then the string**: `lee` becomes `3#lee`.",
  "To decode, read digits up to the next `#` to learn the length, then take exactly that many characters — no matter what they are. Repeat until the end."
 ],
 "code": "class Codec:\n    def encode(self, strs: List[str]) -> str:\n        parts = []\n        for s in strs:\n            parts.append(str(len(s)) + \"#\" + s)\n        return \"\".join(parts)\n\n    def decode(self, s: str) -> List[str]:\n        decoded = []\n        i = 0\n        while i < len(s):\n            delimiter_pos = s.find(\"#\", i)\n            size = int(s[i:delimiter_pos])\n            start_pos = delimiter_pos + 1\n            end_pos = start_pos + size\n            decoded.append(s[start_pos:end_pos])\n            i = end_pos\n        return decoded",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n)",
  "timeWhy": "Both functions touch each character a constant number of times (n = total characters). The pieces are joined once at the end, so nothing is copied again and again.",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "The encoded string and the decoded list are as large as the input."
 },
